  "full_response": "Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are a class of deep neural networks especially suited for processing structured grid data such as images. Here's a general overview of how CNNs process image data:\n\n1. **Input Layer**: \n   - The input to a CNN is an image, which is typically represented as a 2D array (for grayscale images) or a 3D array (for color images) with dimensions corresponding to width, height, and possibly the number of channels (e.g., RGB). \n\n2. **Convolutional Layers**:\n   - **Filter/Kernels**: These layers use a set of learnable filters (kernels) to perform convolution operations on the input image. Each filter slides over the image and computes dot products, creating an output called a feature map. This process captures local features such as edges, textures, or motifs.\n   - **Activation Function**: After each convolution operation, an activation function like ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) is applied. This introduces non-linearities, allowing the network to learn more complex patterns.\n\n3. **Pooling Layers**:\n   - **Downsampling**: Pooling layers reduce the spatial dimensions (width and height) of the feature maps, which helps in reducing the computational load and also controls overfitting. The most common pooling operation is max pooling, where the maximum value in a certain window (e.g., 2x2) is selected.\n   - Pooling operations focus the network on the most significant features and contribute to translational invariance.\n\n4. **Fully Connected Layers**:\n   - After several convolutional and pooling layers, the high-level reasoning in the neural network is performed via fully connected layers. These layers take the feature maps (flattened into vectors) and learn non-linear combinations of these local features to classify the image into various categories.\n   - Each neuron in a fully connected layer connects to all neurons in the previous layer.\n\n5. **Output Layer**:\n   - The final fully connected layer has a number of neurons corresponding to the number of classes the network is designed to recognize (e.g., in a 10-class problem, it would have 10 neurons).\n   - A softmax activation function is often used in the output layer for multi-class classification to convert the raw scores (logits) into probabilities that sum to one.\n\n6. **Training**:\n   - CNNs are typically trained using backpropagation and an optimization algorithm like stochastic gradient descent (SGD). The model learns by adjusting the weights of",
